  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 305 AM EDT Wednesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Warmer with much lower coverage of showers and \nstorms is expected today. \n\nShowers continue across the region early this morning but with much \nlower coverage and intensity than we saw Tuesday afternoon. \nAdditional flooding is not expected. Widespread low stratus clouds \nwill continue through the night across the Piedmont with more breaks \nin the clouds likely for eastern and coastal areas. The closed upper \nlow across north Georgia and the western Carolinas will open into a \ntrough and lift northward today. The front and weak area of low \npressure responsible for all the heavy rainfall will continue to \nlift northward this morning and move away from the area by this \nafternoon. 00z global and CAM guidance both point to much lower \ncoverage of showers today and have limited PoPs to chance/slight \nchance. Forecast soundings show rapid drying aloft and some limited \ninstability so a few isolated storms are possible by this afternoon. \nWidespread flooding is not expected but even a quick half inch or \ninch of precip may be enough to cause localized issues in areas that \nsaw so much rainfall yesterday. Largely for that reason, WPC has \nincluded all but our NE NC counties in a Marginal ERO for today. \nWith fewer showers and clouds, temperatures will rebound into the \nmid and upper 80s with dew points remaining in the low/mid 70s. \nMainly dry but continued warm and muggy tonight with lows in the \n70s.  \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Near normal temperatures and low precip chances \nexpected Thursday into the weekend. \n\nDrying trend continues Thursday with only a very slight chance for a \nstray shower or storm during the afternoon or early evening. The \nvast majority of the area will be dry with mostly sunny to partly \ncloudy skies. High temperatures warm back into the upper 80s and low \n90s (near seasonal norms). Dew points will remain in the 70s but \nwill likely mix into the low 70s during peak afternoon \nheating/mixing, limiting heat indices to the upper 90s and low 100s. \n\nSimilar conditions are expected Friday into the weekend as high \npressure off the SE coast noses westward into the region. Slightly \ngreater chances for diurnally driven showers or storms on Friday and \nSaturday across western and northern portions of the area but PoPs \ngenerally remain aob 40%.",
